{"step_index":108,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T07:46:46Z","content":"Priyanshu, the ₹29,900 is for **Final Cut Pro**, which is Apple's professional video editing software. \n\n**Why it costs 30k:**\nIt is a professional, industry-standard tool. Unlike Adobe Premiere Pro, which forces you to pay a monthly subscription forever, Apple charges a one-time fee. You pay ₹29,900 once, you own it for life, and you get all future updates for free.\n\n**How it helps:**\n1. **Advanced Video Editing:** It gives you advanced timelines, color grading, audio mixing, and motion graphics tools used by YouTubers and filmmakers to create high-end content.\n2. **Maximum Speed:** Because Apple makes both the Mac's M5 chip and Final Cut Pro, the software is highly optimized. It renders 4K and 8K video much faster than third-party editing software and barely drains the battery while doing it.\n\n**Do you actually need it?**\nIf you are not a professional video editor or a serious content creator, **do not buy this.** \n* For basic video trimming and home videos, your Mac already comes with **iMovie** completely free. \n* If you want advanced editing tools without paying 30k, you can download **DaVinci Resolve**, which has a massive free version used by professionals. \n\nUnless you already know your specific workflow requires Final Cut Pro, I recommend unchecking that box and saving your ₹29,900. You can always buy it later from the App Store if you decide you need it.","thinking":"**Analyzing the Cost**\n\nI'm currently focused on dissecting the reasons behind Final Cut Pro's price point. Initial thoughts centered on understanding the user's inquiry about the ~30k price. I've begun to zero in on FCP as the core subject, aiming to craft a clear explanation. My goal now is to articulate why it is so expensive and what it can do.\n\n\n**Defining the Purpose**\n\nI've clarified that Final Cut Pro (FCP) is Apple's professional video editing software for Mac. The ₹29,900 price tag is a one-time purchase, unlike Adobe Premiere Pro's subscription model. It's a forever license including future updates. I've also identified its core functionalities: video editing, color grading, audio mixing, motion graphics, and fast rendering, especially on M-series chips. I'm noting its target audience of professionals and filmmakers, highlighting iMovie, DaVinci Resolve (free) and CapCut as alternatives for casual users.\n\n\n"}
