{"step_index":114,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T07:49:42Z","content":"Priyanshu, **Yes, absolutely.**\n\nThe Final Cut Pro license is tied to your **Apple ID account**, not the physical laptop. It does not matter if the MacBook is new, secondhand, or if the previous owner never bought it. \n\nOnce you buy the secondhand MacBook:\n1. You will log in with your own Apple ID.\n2. Open the Mac App Store.\n3. Search for Final Cut Pro and buy it for ₹29,900.\n\nFrom that moment on, the software belongs to *you* for life. If you sell that secondhand MacBook a few years later and buy a brand new one, you can just log into your new Mac and download Final Cut Pro again for free because it is permanently attached to your account.","thinking":"**Assessing the Scenario**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the core of the user's question: can they purchase the Final Cut Pro license under these specific secondhand purchase conditions? This involves a careful examination of Apple's licensing rules and their applicability in this unique situation. I'm focusing on the \"never bought\" aspect of the original owner.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Purchase**\n\nI've clarified the critical aspect: Final Cut Pro is inextricably linked to the Apple ID, not the MacBook's hardware. Regardless of its secondhand status or the previous owner's purchase history, the user can buy it from the Mac App Store using their own Apple ID. The anti-sycophancy rules are being followed to ensure clarity and actionable steps.\n\n\n"}
